# 06 — Strings & string.h

## What is this?
C strings are null-terminated (`\0`) char arrays.

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.c`:
1. `size_t custom_strlen(const char *str)`: Calculates length without `<string.h>`.
2. `int count_char_occurrences(const char *str, char target)`: Counts occurrences of `target`.
3. `void reverse_string(char *str)`: Reverses string in place.

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```c
#include <stddef.h>

size_t custom_strlen(const char *str) {
    if (str == 0) return 0;
    size_t len = 0;
    while (str[len] != '\0') len++;
    return len;
}

int count_char_occurrences(const char *str, char target) {
    if (str == 0) return 0;
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; str[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (str[i] == target) count++;
    }
    return count;
}

void reverse_string(char *str) {
    if (str == 0) return;
    int len = 0;
    while (str[len] != '\0') len++;
    for (int i = 0; i < len / 2; i++) {
        char temp = str[i];
        str[i] = str[len - 1 - i];
        str[len - 1 - i] = temp;
    }
}
```
</details>

