# 07 — Dynamic Memory (malloc & free)

## What is this?
Dynamic allocation requests memory from heap via `malloc` and deallocates with `free`.

## What to Practice
Implement in `exercise.c`:
1. `int* create_range(int start, int count)`: Allocates `count` integers from `start` to `start+count-1` (NULL if `count <= 0`).
2. `int sum_and_free(int *arr, int count)`: Sums elements, frees `arr`, returns sum.
3. `int* duplicate_array(const int *src, int count)`: Allocates and copies `src`.

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int* create_range(int start, int count) {
    if (count <= 0) return NULL;
    int *arr = (int *)malloc(count * sizeof(int));
    if (arr == NULL) return NULL;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        arr[i] = start + i;
    }
    return arr;
}

int sum_and_free(int *arr, int count) {
    if (arr == NULL || count <= 0) {
        if (arr != NULL) free(arr);
        return 0;
    }
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        sum += arr[i];
    }
    free(arr);
    return sum;
}

int* duplicate_array(const int *src, int count) {
    if (src == NULL || count <= 0) return NULL;
    int *copy = (int *)malloc(count * sizeof(int));
    if (copy == NULL) return NULL;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        copy[i] = src[i];
    }
    return copy;
}
```
</details>

