# 09 — Unions & Enums

## What is this?
An `enum` defines named integer constants. A `union` shares memory between members.

## What to Practice
Given:
```c
typedef enum { TYPE_INT, TYPE_FLOAT } ValueType;
typedef union { int i_val; float f_val; } NumberUnion;
typedef struct { ValueType type; NumberUnion value; } TaggedNumber;
```
Implement in `exercise.c`:
1. `TaggedNumber create_int_tagged(int n)`: Initializes with `TYPE_INT`.
2. `TaggedNumber create_float_tagged(float f)`: Initializes with `TYPE_FLOAT`.
3. `double to_double(TaggedNumber num)`: Converts to double.

## Reference Solution

<details>
<summary>Click to reveal reference solution</summary>

```c
typedef enum { TYPE_INT, TYPE_FLOAT } ValueType;
typedef union { int i_val; float f_val; } NumberUnion;
typedef struct { ValueType type; NumberUnion value; } TaggedNumber;

TaggedNumber create_int_tagged(int n) {
    TaggedNumber res;
    res.type = TYPE_INT;
    res.value.i_val = n;
    return res;
}

TaggedNumber create_float_tagged(float f) {
    TaggedNumber res;
    res.type = TYPE_FLOAT;
    res.value.f_val = f;
    return res;
}

double to_double(TaggedNumber num) {
    if (num.type == TYPE_INT) return (double)num.value.i_val;
    return (double)num.value.f_val;
}
```
</details>

